now at the Kubernetes

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:07.640
Whack-a-Pod booth with Mandy.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:08.620
Mandy, how are you?

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.286
MANDY WAITE: I'm
pretty good, thank you.

00:11:10.286 --> 00:11:11.020
How's it going?

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:12.810
TIMOTHY JORDAN: It's fantastic.

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:16.750
So just a moment ago, Drea and
I were playing Whack-a-Pod,

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:17.950
and it was a lot of fun.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.320
But could you tell us what
we were doing while we

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:21.845
were whacking those pods?

00:11:21.845 --> 00:11:23.470
MANDY WAITE: Yeah,
we're whacking pods.

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:26.260
So these pods are
parts of the components

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:29.350
of an application that are
running within Kubernetes.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:31.600
We have-- this set
up is a little bit

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.740
kind of geared towards the
experience specifically.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:39.170
But the idea is a pod is a
component of your application.

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.950
It can run anywhere [INAUDIBLE]
cluster in Kubernetes.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:43.240
It can be scheduled anywhere.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:44.210
It could go down.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.510
If it goes down, it
comes back up again.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:49.476
It will be managed
effectively by Kubernetes

00:11:49.476 --> 00:11:51.850
to make sure that we always
have the right number of pods

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:52.627
at any given time.

00:11:52.627 --> 00:11:54.460
In this case, we have
moles instead of pods.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:57.290
But it's pretty
much the same thing.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:11:59.680
TIMOTHY JORDAN: So I
think the key thing here

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:02.800
is that as any of them go
down, others are coming up.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:04.630
And so it's like kind
of a losing battle

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:06.510
to try and bring
these servers down.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.149
Not servers, pods, excuse me.

00:12:08.149 --> 00:12:09.440
MANDY WAITE: Not servers, yeah.

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:11.606
We could do the same with
the servers, to be honest.

00:12:11.606 --> 00:12:13.464
We can bring the
servers back up as well.

00:12:13.464 --> 00:12:14.630
So we could do auto-scaling.

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:16.588
We can have managed
instance groups that manage

00:12:16.588 --> 00:12:18.700
the nodes as well as pods.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:20.930
But pods are very
small, ephemeral units

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:22.580
of your running application.

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:25.870
They're designed to be
ephemeral and replaceable.

00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:27.517
So therefore, when
one goes away,

00:12:27.517 --> 00:12:29.350
another one will come
back up to replace it,

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:30.933
absolutely identical,
and traffic will

00:12:30.933 --> 00:12:32.530
start to be routed to it again.

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.029
DREA ALPHONSO: And how would
businesses without Kubernetes--

00:12:35.029 --> 00:12:36.765
how would they handle
this without that?

00:12:36.765 --> 00:12:38.140
MANDY WAITE: So
basically this is

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:39.430
a kind of different
way of thinking,

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:42.100
a different way of thinking--
we've been working with Google

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:45.940
for the last 10 or so years
through something called Borg.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:47.740
And basically what
people would do,

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:49.190
they would find they
have lots of containers.

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:50.470
They would have
to manage systems.

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:52.750
And they would have to manage
the container run times

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:53.990
independently.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.660
There are other alternatives to
Kubernetes out there as well.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.118
They can manage
containers for you.

00:12:58.118 --> 00:12:59.827
But we think
Kubernetes is probably

00:12:59.827 --> 00:13:00.910
the one you should choose.

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:02.320
DREA ALPHONSO: Right.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:04.810
TIMOTHY JORDAN: Could we do
it, and maybe you could tell us

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:06.220
what's happening on the screen?

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:07.470
MANDY WAITE: Absolutely, yeah.

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:08.220
Let's go.

00:13:10.844 --> 00:13:12.010
DREA ALPHONSO: I'm whacking.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:14.737
TIMOTHY JORDAN: So maybe we'll
just have Drea whack some pods.

00:13:14.737 --> 00:13:17.070
MANDY WAITE: OK, so Drea is
currently whacking the pods.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:18.792
The pods are going down.

00:13:18.792 --> 00:13:20.950
They have big hash names.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:23.740
So you can see each
name is unique.

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.090
When they go down, they go gray.

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:27.173
When they're coming back
up again, they go orange.

00:13:27.173 --> 00:13:28.610
Then they'll become blue again.

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.370
Maybe if you do it
fast enough you'll

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:32.530
be able to take the
entire service down,

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:34.412
but not for very long.

00:13:34.412 --> 00:13:36.370
So if you can imagine
somebody is playing havoc

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:39.890
with your systems,
taking your pods down,

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:41.950
Kubernetes will [INAUDIBLE]
for you and make sure

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:43.616
you always have the
right number of pods

00:13:43.616 --> 00:13:45.117
running at any given time.

00:13:45.117 --> 00:13:47.200
TIMOTHY JORDAN: That's a
pretty great score, Drea.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:49.420
But when we were doing it
together, it was even better.

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:50.340
DREA ALPHONSO: Oh,
yeah, all right, we did.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:51.810
I think we hit, like, 1,000.

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:53.560
MANDY WAITE: So yeah,
so that's it really.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:54.070
TIMOTHY JORDAN: Is
there anything else

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:55.778
you want to say about
Kubernetes or where

00:13:55.778 --> 00:13:56.962
