00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:41.760
We're here with Kaz.

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:44.301
Kaz would you mind introducing
yourself and telling everybody

00:14:44.301 --> 00:14:45.280
out there what you do?

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:45.790
KAZ SATO: Thanks so much.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:46.500
I'm Kaz.

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.380
I'm a developer advocate
for Google Cloud.

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:52.270
And here we have the so-called
Find Your Candy demonstrations,

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:55.450
where you can tell some
voice command to the tablet

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:58.260
so that the robot can
understand your voice command

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:00.580
and try to find the
best candy for you

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:03.470
and pick it up by
recognizing the image.

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:04.990
TIMOTHY JORDAN: So
there's a number

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:06.490
of different machine
learning algorithms

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.364
going on there, both in
the voice recognition

00:15:08.364 --> 00:15:11.849
and understanding not just what
you said, but what it means,

00:15:11.849 --> 00:15:13.140
as well as selecting the candy.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.840
Can you walk us
through that flow?

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:16.360
KAZ SATO: Yeah,
so initially, it's

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:19.870
using the Voice Speech API
to recognize your voice.

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:22.990
And then it uses the Natural
Language API to understand

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:25.090
the words inside the sentence.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:27.760
And then it uses
cloud machine learning

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:31.120
to use the algorithms
called [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.640
[INAUDIBLE] is an algorithm
used to understand

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:35.260
the meaning of each word.

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.110
So it understands the
similarity between hot

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:41.140
and spicy or creamy and milky.

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:44.350
And then it tries to use the
[? word ?] to choose the best

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:45.650
candies to pick up.

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:49.380
And finally, it uses the cloud
ML again to recognize the--

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:53.170
to the image recognition
on each object.

00:15:53.170 --> 00:15:55.480
TIMOTHY JORDAN: That's a
lot of machine learning.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:57.350
KAZ SATO: Yes, tons of it.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.170
TIMOTHY JORDAN: All
right, shall we try it?

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:01.790
KAZ SATO: Yeah, you can try it.

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:03.080
DREA ALPHONSO: Let's see.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:07.779
I like mints, oh,
voice recognition.

00:16:07.779 --> 00:16:08.820
TIMOTHY JORDAN: I see it.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:10.930
It's finding the
whole sentence there,

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.020
it knows what each
of those words is.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.770
I bet it's going to pick
out the noun, right?

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:16.490
KAZ SATO: Yes.

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:19.135
So it understands all
of the parts of speech,

00:16:19.135 --> 00:16:21.080
like a noun, an adjective.

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:23.199
And now it's choosing
the candies for you.

00:16:23.199 --> 00:16:24.990
TIMOTHY JORDAN: Oh,
look, it chooses mints.

00:16:27.059 --> 00:16:28.350
